# Response headers for the Pages site.
#
# The Content-Security-Policy is the point of this file. The page carries a
# password field, and a login page is judged partly on how tightly it is
# locked down: no inline scripts, nothing loaded from anywhere unexpected,
# and — the one that matters most for a login — form-action 'self', which
# means the browser itself will refuse to post these credentials anywhere
# but this origin. Keeping it strict is also just correct.
#
# Whenever a new external source is added (a font host, an analytics script,
# a CDN), it has to be listed here or the browser blocks it silently.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data:; media-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' wss://rockpaperwizards.blipgaming.ca; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

# The game's own code, and why it is not cached hard.
#
# index.html is always revalidated, so the ?v= it points at is always the
# current one. src/*.js used to be handed out with four hours of browser cache
# on top of that, on the reasoning that the ?v= makes the URL unique per deploy.
# It only does when somebody remembers to change it. Reuse a version — or hold a
# copy from before one — and two players end up running different programs while
# both truthfully reporting the same version. The match starts and comes apart a
# few seconds later, looking exactly like a bad connection. It is not one.
#
# These four files total well under 300KB and revalidate to a 304. The audio and
# the pictures, which cannot affect the simulation, keep the long cache.
#
# THIS RULE DOES NOTHING. Kept only so the next person does not re-add it.
#
# Measured on the live deployment, fetching files INSIDE this rule and OUTSIDE
# it as a control:
#
#   blipgaming.ca   /src/game.js  14400   /assets/audio/*  14400   /favicon.svg  14400
#   pages.dev       /src/game.js      0   /assets/audio/*      0   /favicon.svg      0
#
# The value tracks the SURFACE, not the rule: everything non-HTML is 4h on the
# custom domain and 0 on pages.dev, whether this rule covers it or not. Pages
# ignores Cache-Control from _headers for its static assets. (Everything else in
# this file works — the CSP above is served on both domains — so the file is
# deployed and read; it is this directive specifically that is dropped.)
#
# Sampling only /src/* makes pages.dev look like the rule is working. It is not:
# check a path the rule does NOT cover before believing it.
#
# If the four-hour window is ever worth closing, the levers are a Cache Rule on
# the zone, or a Pages Function (_headers does not apply to Function responses).
# Neither is needed today: the build fingerprint catches a mismatched client and
# says so before the match starts.
/src/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Never let an account response sit in a cache.
/api/*
  Cache-Control: no-store
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
